course: world
lesson: 70
title: Stars, planets and constellations in the night sky
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: On a dark, clear night far from city lights you can see a few thousand stars with your eyes alone; in a city, the glow of the lights hides most of them.
explain: The stars seem to move slowly across the sky during the night, rising in the east and setting in the west, but it is really the Earth turning, just as it makes the Sun rise and set.
explain: In the Northern Hemisphere one star, Polaris, the North Star, hardly moves at all, because it sits almost straight above the Earth's North Pole, and all the other stars seem to circle around it.
explain: The stars in a constellation only look close together: they lie in about the same direction from us, but some are many times farther away than others.
explain: A planet looks like a bright star, but over weeks it slowly wanders among the constellations, because it is much nearer and goes around the Sun; the word planet comes from the Greek for wanderer.
explain: For example, the brightest point in the evening sky after the Moon is often Venus, called the evening star, though it is a planet that shines by reflecting sunlight.
fact: On a dark, clear night a person can see a few thousand stars without a telescope.
fact: The stars seem to move across the sky at night because the Earth turns.
fact: Polaris, the North Star, stays almost still in the sky, and the other stars seem to circle around it.
fact: The stars of a constellation are usually at very different distances from us.
fact: Astronomers divide the whole sky into 88 constellations.
fact: Planets slowly change their place among the stars from week to week.
fact: Stars twinkle because moving air bends their light this way and that; planets usually shine more steadily.
fact: We see different constellations in winter and in summer because the Earth travels around the Sun.
fact: People in the Southern Hemisphere see stars that people in the far north never see, such as the Southern Cross.
fact: The Milky Way, the pale band across a dark sky, is the light of countless far-away stars of our own galaxy.
fact: A shooting star is not a star but a meteor: a speck of space rock burning up in the air.
fact: The stars are still in the sky during the day, but the bright sunlit sky hides them.
exercise: Why do the stars seem to move across the sky during the night? | Because the Earth turns | Because the stars fly around the Earth | Because the wind blows them | Because the Moon pulls them along
exercise: Which star stays almost still in the northern sky? | Polaris, the North Star | The brightest star in the sky | The Sun | Any star near the Moon
exercise: Why can you see more stars in the countryside than in a city? | City lights brighten the sky and hide faint stars | There are fewer stars above cities than above the countryside | The air in the countryside is closer to space | Stars only shine above fields and forests
exercise: How far apart are the stars in a constellation? | They are often at very different distances from us | They always touch each other | They are all exactly the same distance away from the Earth | They are all inside our solar system
exercise: How can you tell a planet from a star over several weeks? | The planet slowly moves among the stars | The planet always twinkles much more than stars | The planet only appears in the sky in the daytime | The planet is always bright red in colour
exercise: What does the word planet mean in Greek? | Wanderer | Fire | Bright light | Round stone
exercise: Why do stars twinkle? | Moving air in the atmosphere bends their light | The stars switch their light on and off very quickly | The stars are spinning very fast | Clouds pass in front of each star
exercise: What is a shooting star? | A speck of space rock burning up in the air | A star falling out of the sky and landing far away | A star exploding far away | A planet moving very fast
exercise: What is the pale band of the Milky Way across a dark sky? | The light of countless far-away stars of our galaxy | A long thin cloud lit by the Moon | Dust blown up from the deserts | The path left by an aeroplane
exercise: Why can we not see stars in the daytime? | The bright sunlit sky hides them | The stars go out during the day | The stars move to the other side of the Sun | The clouds always cover them
exercise: Why do we see different constellations in summer and in winter? | The Earth travels around the Sun | The stars move to new places every season | The Moon hides some of them in summer | The air is clearer in winter
exercise: What is the bright evening star often seen after sunset? | The planet Venus | The North Star | A very close comet | The International Space Station
exercise: About how many constellations is the whole sky divided into? | 88 | 12 | 8 | 1,000
exercise: Which stars can people in Australia see that people in Norway never see? | The Southern Cross | The North Star | The Sun | The planets
test: You take a long-exposure photo (the camera stays open for hours) of the northern sky. The stars make circles. Which star is near the centre? | Polaris, the North Star | Venus | The Southern Cross | The Sun
test: Two stars look side by side in the same constellation. Which is true? | One may be much farther from us than the other | They must be exactly the same distance away from us | They must be touching each other | They must be the same size
test: A bright point does not twinkle and has moved compared with the stars since last month. What is it probably? | A planet | A shooting star | A very young star | A distant galaxy
test: A friend makes a wish on a shooting star. What did she really see? | A tiny piece of space rock burning up | A star falling all the way to the Earth | A brand new star being born in the sky | The reflected light of a far-away planet
test: If the Earth stopped turning, what would happen to the stars in the night sky? | They would stop rising and setting | They would spin twice as fast | They would all disappear | They would all fall down to the ground
test: In a big city at night you see only a handful of stars. What is the best reason? | The city lights make the sky too bright | The air above a city has no stars | The warm city air blocks starlight | The tall buildings block every one of them
test: Which is true about the stars during the day? | They are still there, but the sunlight hides them | They move under the ground until night comes again | They switch off until evening | They all hide behind the Sun
test: Why would a traveller from Ukraine notice new constellations in South Africa? | Part of the southern sky cannot be seen from the far north | The stars change on every continent | The sky is closer to the ground in Africa | South Africa has its own separate stars
test: An astronaut on the Moon, with no air around, looks at the stars. What does she notice? | The stars do not twinkle | The stars twinkle much more | The stars are all red | There are no stars at all
test: why's the north star always in the same spot | Polaris stays almost still, and the other stars seem to circle round it | Polaris is the closest star and does not move | Polaris is a planet and so it never moves | Polaris moves, but too fast for us to see
test: where do the stars go in the daytime | They are still there, but the bright sky hides them | They move to the other side of the Sun | They go out in the morning and light up again at night | They hide behind the Moon until dark
test: Ava says a shooting star is a star falling down. true? | No, it is a speck of space rock burning up in the air | Yes, it is a small star dropping out of the sky | Yes, but it burns out before it reaches us | No, it is a planet moving very fast
test: my sister reckons the stars in a constellation are all close together. right? | No, they are usually at very different distances from us | Yes, they sit side by side in a small group, close together | Yes, they are held together by a cloud of gas | No, they are all at exactly the same distance
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the stars appear to move across the sky at night. | The rotation of the Earth | The motion of the stars around the Sun | The motion of the Moon across the sky | The wind in the upper atmosphere
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a shooting star? | A speck of space rock burning in the air | A star falling towards the Earth | A planet passing in front of the Moon | A satellite returning to the Earth
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the stars during the daytime. | They remain in the sky but are concealed by the bright sunlit sky | They are extinguished each morning and rekindled at night | They move to the opposite side of the Sun | They descend below the horizon all together
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which different constellations are seen in winter and in summer. | The Earth travels around the Sun | The stars travel around the Earth | The Moon conceals certain stars in summer | The stars change their shapes with the seasons
